# Building — reviewer 3 (falsifiability)
VERDICT: FINDINGS
COUNT: 23
## Findings
1. Notice + Solve Inefficiencies — no artifact reveals an inefficiency the agent noticed but silently absorbed — claim “no actionable inefficiency observed” while repeatedly routing around the same 40-minute manual process.
2. Friction Is a Spec — recurrence and “likely to recur” are neither recorded nor thresholded — call each repeated workaround a one-off and never file or productize it.
3. Iterative Audit to Convergence — nothing observes that audit-shaped work occurred unless the agent opens a canonical audit record — perform one sweep, label the task a review, and report it complete without invoking the convergence guard.
4. One Failure Teaches Every Guard — the fingerprint machinery cannot observe an incident that was never entered as an enforcement gap, nor require remediation of a recorded match — patch the immediate failure, claim it was not a guard failure, and leave every structurally similar guard untouched.
5. A Metric Must Measure the Work, Not the Question — outside canonical audit records, neither the measurement question nor its subject is captured — paraphrase the same repair-chase prompt, call it a different question, and credit the falling count as convergence.
6. LLM-Supervised Execution — neither “critical” nor a supervisor’s required evidence and rejection conditions are defined; `Earned from` names no checkable pipeline or incident — set `supervision: tier1` while the supervisor unconditionally approves every step without inspecting its output.
7. Tiered Development — no observation makes a below-floor declaration incorrect because the article expressly permits it and supplies no audit threshold — declare a high-risk fleet migration Tier 1, record plausible reasoning, and take the light path while claiming the audited choice satisfies the standard.
8. Conservative Outbound: Act, Don't Notify — “genuinely requires the human” is accepted through authored justification rather than an outcome test — classify routine progress narration as an anomaly the user must know and ship the emitter enabled by default.
9. Scrape/Parser Fixture Realness — the lint can verify loader, sidecar, and test structure but cannot prove bytes came from a real capture — hand-author a realistically messy fixture, add provenance metadata, and claim byte-faithful capture while testing against invented input.
10. Migration-Consumer Completeness — the gate observes only declared consumers and matching revision markers — omit a legacy authorization reader from the contract, update every declared consumer, and claim complete migration while production still reads the retired authority.
11. An Instar Agent Is Always a Multi-Machine Entity — the deterministic guard grades posture-marker form, not correspondence with storage behavior — mark a feature `unified` while writing its durable state only to a machine-local path.
12. Expected Capacity Enforcement Is an Outcome, Not a Degradation — the lint detects contracts, markers, result types, and revision alignment, not whether discarded data is within policy — declare a truncation successful while dropping an essential field, return `CapacityEnforcementResult`, and emit no degradation.
13. A Decision That Can Block Must Live Where the Checks Can See It — an already-declared blocking file may change without the ratchet inspecting its decision semantics — materially weaken a shell refusal, retain its existing declaration, and claim the blocking population remains controlled.
14. A Refusal Stays a Refusal — the build ratchet observes `SessionRouter`, not every adapter and result boundary covered by the Rule — preserve `rejected` in the router but map it to `{ok:true}` in a different transport adapter and claim the pinned routing test proves conservation.
15. Compaction Parity — the parity test observes matching `*/session-context` fetch syntax, not effective recovery — place the matching fetch in an unreachable branch or discard its result and claim start/compaction parity while the session forgets the context.
16. Bounded Blast Radius — the named lint and burst proof detect funneled LLM subprocesses, not every subprocess, memory allocator, or physical resource named by the Rule — add an uncapped non-LLM worker fan-out and claim compliance because the spawn-provider lint and semaphore test remain green.
17. An Autonomous Run Must Outlive Its Session — the article protects only a “registered” run but never makes registration observable or mandatory for delegated autonomous work — omit registration, let the vessel reap the run, and claim no protected run failed.
18. Framework-Agnostic — and Framework-Optimizing — the enforcement surface covers session launch/inject/resume and declarations about two engines, not off-surface behavior — add a Codex-only parser or storage feature, avoid session lifecycle code, and claim framework parity because all guarded registries and review fields pass.
19. Zero-Failure — gate-time runs cannot distinguish continuous greenness from a red suite introduced and repaired between gates; `Earned from` is only a general responsibility story — break tests on an unpushed branch, repair them before the next run, and claim the suite was “green at all times.”
20. Test Identity Never Enters Production State — the guard recognizes only the maintained marker set, whose completeness has no test — create a synthetic owner named outside that set, write it into production state, and claim the registry validator found no test identity.
21. Decision Provenance & Outcome Review — no stated guard compares logged context with the actual arbiter input or decides when ground truth exists — scrub decision-bearing fields as “sensitive,” declare the remaining record complete, and classify every inconvenient case as lacking ground truth.
22. Stall Coverage Is Enumerated, Not Discovered — resolvable symbols plus a collected raw-signature test do not prove the production framework path invokes either symbol — test detector and recovery functions directly, mark the cell `covered`, but leave runtime wiring absent.
23. Observability — you can't tune what you can't see — no feature inventory or adequacy criterion distinguishes useful full-loop metrics from decorative counters — ship an `attempted` counter that increments before work, omit outcome/use/correction measurement, and claim the feature is auditable because `/metrics` exposes a number.
